<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bakhchysarai Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Khan's Palace</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhchysarai">Bakhchysarai</a> Palace, or Khan's Palace, is the residence of the Crimean khans at Bakhchysarai, begun in the 16th century. Its courtyards, mosques, harem, cemetery and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhchisaray-fountain">Fountain of Tears</a>, which inspired a poem by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Pushkin</a>, reflect Ottoman and Persian influence. Largely rebuilt after Russian troops burned it in 1736, it is the main surviving monument of the Crimean Khanate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bakhchysarai-palace.jpg" alt="Bakhchysarai Palace" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chapultepec · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hansaray1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
